speechtext:
1.	What revenue was received from April 1,
1931, to January 1, 1932, by the Post Office Department from:	(a) postage stamps, post
cards, etc.; (b) paid in cash on newspapers;
(c)	paid in cash on third class printed matter;
(d)	paid in cash on metered mail matter; (e) paid in cash on parcels?
2.	What revenue was received from the above for the last nine months of the fiscal year ending March 31, 1930?

1.	To what provinces has the Dominion government made loans during the fiscal year endinn March 31. next?
2.	What is:	(a) the aggregate amount of
such loan or loans to each province; (b) the respective dates upon which they were made;
(c)	the dates upon which they become due;
(d)	the interest charged thereon; (e) the security taken therefor; (f) the purpose or purposes for which they were made; and (g) the source from which the Dominion Minister of Finance secured the necessary money to make such loans to the provinces?

Yes, I know hon. members laugh, but let them laugh at the man who made the statement. I am simply stating his argument; I am not making the statement;
I am laughing at it. I always thought education was power; that education meant the development of mental power. It would appear that as yet the hon. member for Marquette does not understand that. If a man has not some mental power, how is he going to adjust himself to mixed farming, live stock raising, producing pigs, horses, mules, jack asses or anything else? It cannot be done. We shall have to leave it to the hon. member for Marquette to make a success of these various activities.
Perhaps I had better get down to saying something about the resolution. After the theatrical performance we have had from Long Lake and Marquette this afternoon on making the production of mules, asses, other live stock and so forth, attractive or profitable, it might be well to tell the house that so far as the constituency which I have the honour to represent is concerned, we grow wheat, we grow practically every other cereal product; we are in the live stock business; we are in dairying; we have lumbering and fishing and we have every requirement in the way of building up a district that is possessed by any other part of Canada.
